DAB DSP and original FIC/TII, label and MOT code: local dab-monitor,
revision ec7ced2e9619eab2c55c97ca59aaea9aa2aef4f5.
Imported 2026-11-06; maintained here as project-owned GPL-3.0-only sources.
The source repository has no standalone LICENSE.

The streaming radio extension, dx_msc and dx_pad were implemented here from
published protocol specifications and existing project DSP primitives.
The previous AbracaDABra libdabsdr 4.0.1 adapter, headers and binaries were
removed from the current tree; earlier Git revisions retain their notices.
No new receiver source was imported from AbracaDABra or OnAir for this
extension. Reference receivers were inspected during investigation: this is
an implementation provenance record, not a claim of legal clean-room isolation.
The generic OnAir resampler remains reused from components/dvbt2, with its
original GPL notices and UPSTREAM.json. AAC decoding uses external FAAD2,
GPL-2.0-or-later: https://github.com/knik0/faad2
Code from FAAD2 is copyright (c) Nero AG, www.nero.com

Protocol sources (versions deliberately pinned, PDFs not bundled):
ETSI EN 300 401 V2.1.1 (2017-01)
https://www.etsi.org/deliver/etsi_en/300400_300499/300401/02.01.01_60/en_300401v020101p.pdf
- 6.2/6.3/6.4: subchannels, components and reconfiguration.
- 7.4: PAD contents indicators, data groups, CRC and dynamic labels.
- 10: energy dispersal; 11.1/11.3.2, tables 13/18/20: convolution and EEP.
- 12/table 21: time interleaving; 14: Mode-I OFDM, mapping and modulation.
ETSI TS 102 563 V2.1.1 (2017-01)
https://www.etsi.org/deliver/etsi_ts/102500_102599/102563/02.01.01_60/ts_102563v020101p.pdf
- 5.1/5.2: AAC parameters, superframe structure, firecode and AU CRC.
- 5.4: leading AAC data-stream element carrying PAD.
- 6: shortened RS(120,110); annexes C/D: superframe synchronization/processing.
ETSI TS 102 980 V2.1.1 (2017-01), 7.1: DL Plus command transport.
https://www.etsi.org/deliver/etsi_ts/102900_102999/102980/02.01.01_60/ts_102980v020101p.pdf

Implementation choices, not mandated by ETSI: bounded queues and recovery,
soft reliability weighting, local CP timing tracking, fourth-power phase
tracking, CRC-selected quadrant, Viterbi survivor storage, Berlekamp-Massey
with Chien search and a small linear system for RS magnitudes, and a 5 ms
PCM ramp on loss/recovery. No comparative performance advantage is claimed.
Tests use synthetic transmitter vectors plus optional existing recorded IQ;
the recordings and any broadcast audio are not distributed with the sources.

Existing DX research reference, checked against dab-monitor/docs/734135.pdf:
W.J. van Houtum (2012), Two-dimensional block-based reception for
Differentially Encoded OFDM Systems, TU/e, sections 6.2--6.4 and 7.3,
equation 7.18. https://doi.org/10.6100/IR734135
This underlies the pre-existing non-iterative 8x4 FIC fallback, not the
continuous audio demodulator. The earlier shorthand title in the source
comment was corrected to the thesis's actual bibliographic title.

Also consulted in dab-monitor: docs/fic-decoder-investigation.md and decisions
0007 (PRS candidates), 0008 (CRC-valid identity accumulation), 0013 (temporal
candidate ranking), 0019 (2D fallback). Their key constraint is retained:
OFDM timing, a CRC-valid FIB, ECC+EId and confirmed TII are separate evidence
levels. Do not wait for audio or a label before reporting protocol identity;
do not equate a short lock or synthetic-noise rescue with verified DX gain.
The local Steven thesis 374696-1.pdf (1992) was inspected as historical COFDM
background; it supplies no constants or code in this extension.
